Chapter 2
Common-Size Statements
القوائم المالية بالنسب المئوية
A balance sheet of $31,672,600 and a balance sheet of $310,000,000 cannot be compared line by line. One is a hundred times the other, and every difference you see is mostly a difference of size. Turn both into percentages of their own totals and the size disappears, leaving only the shape: what proportion of the company is inventory, how much of it the owners paid for, how much of each sales dollar survives to the bottom.
That is a common-size statement, and it is the first thing an analyst makes. It is also called vertical analysis, because the percentages run down the column.
Figure F502-01 is your map.
Figure F502-01. Chapter 2 roadmap: two statements, two bases, one shape.
