WHAT YOU ALREADY KNOW   What you already know
You already know that the balance sheet totals to total assets on one side and to liabilities plus equity on the other, and that the income statement runs from net sales down to net income. A common-size statement changes nothing in either. It adds a column.
2.1  One total as a hundred
A common-size statement sets one line of the statement to 100 per cent and expresses every other line as a percentage of it. The choice of base is fixed by convention, and the exam expects it.
For the balance sheet the base is total assets. Every asset, every liability and every equity line is divided by total assets. Because assets equal liabilities plus equity, the liability and equity side also adds to 100 per cent, so the two sides remain equal in percentage terms as they were in dollars.
For the income statement the base is net sales. Every cost, every subtotal and net income itself is divided by net sales. The result is read as what happens to each sales dollar: so many cents go to the cost of the goods, so many to selling and administration, so many survive as profit.
EXAM TRAP   The base is not negotiable
Balance sheet: total assets. Not total equity, not current assets, not the largest line.
Income statement: net sales. Not gross profit, not total revenue including other income, not net income.
When a question gives you other income or interest income, those are expressed against net sales too, even though they are not sales.
A common-size statement for one year, standing alone, tells you the shape of the company. Two years side by side tell you whether the shape is changing, and that is usually the more useful reading. Both appear in the figures below.
