2.2  The common-size balance sheet
Figure F502-02 is Orontes's balance sheet from Part 1, with a percentage column beside each year. Every percentage is the dollar amount divided by total assets for that year.
Figure F502-02. Orontes Foods, Inc. — common-size balance sheets at December 31 (percentage of total assets)
@table
Orontes Foods Inc. (whole USD) | 2025 | % | 2024 | %
Cash | 7,302,100 | 23.1 | 2,100,000 | 7.9
Trading securities | 42,000 | 0.1 | 40,000 | 0.2
Accounts receivable, net | 4,144,000 | 13.1 | 3,760,000 | 14.2
Inventory | 2,450,000 | 7.7 | 2,300,000 | 8.7
Prepaid expenses | 204,000 | 0.6 | 180,000 | 0.7
Assets held for sale | 46,000 | 0.1 | — | —
Total current assets | 14,188,100 | 44.8 | 8,380,000 | 31.7
Debt securities | 88,500 | 0.3 | 90,000 | 0.3
Equity securities | 27,000 | 0.1 | 25,000 | 0.1
Equity-method investments | 1,807,000 | 5.7 | 500,000 | 1.9
Property, plant and equipment, net | 14,802,000 | 46.7 | 16,000,000 | 60.5
Brand (indefinite-lived) | 260,000 | 0.8 | 300,000 | 1.1
Goodwill | 300,000 | 0.9 | 950,000 | 3.6
Other noncurrent assets | 200,000 | 0.6 | 200,000 | 0.8
TOTAL ASSETS | 31,672,600 | 100.0 | 26,445,000 | 100.0
Accounts payable | 2,050,000 | 6.5 | 1,900,000 | 7.2
Accrued liabilities | 900,000 | 2.8 | 850,000 | 3.2
Income taxes payable | 350,000 | 1.1 | 300,000 | 1.1
Contract liabilities | 180,000 | 0.6 | 120,000 | 0.5
Dividends payable | 400,000 | 1.3 | — | —
Current portion of long-term debt | 400,000 | 1.3 | 400,000 | 1.5
Total current liabilities | 4,280,000 | 13.5 | 3,570,000 | 13.5
Long-term debt, less current portion | 6,000,000 | 18.9 | 5,600,000 | 21.2
Deferred tax liability | 999,625 | 3.2 | 900,000 | 3.4
Total liabilities | 11,279,625 | 35.6 | 10,070,000 | 38.1
Common stock, $1 par | 8,100,000 | 25.6 | 8,000,000 | 30.3
Additional paid-in capital | 5,400,000 | 17.0 | 4,000,000 | 15.1
Retained earnings | 6,894,100 | 21.8 | 4,375,000 | 16.5
Total stockholders' equity | 20,392,975 | 64.4 | 16,375,000 | 61.9
TOTAL LIABILITIES AND EQUITY | 31,672,600 | 100.0 | 26,445,000 | 100.0
@end
Four things in that column are worth naming, and each one becomes a chapter later.
The asset mix moved. Current assets were 31.7 per cent of the company and are now 44.8 per cent. Property, plant and equipment fell from 60.5 per cent to 46.7 per cent. Orontes did not sell a factory: the dollar amount of PP&E fell by only $1,198,000, while total assets grew. Most of the shift is cash.
Cash tripled as a proportion. It was 7.9 per cent of total assets and is now 23.1 per cent. Nearly a quarter of this company is now cash. That is either a company storing up for something, or a company that cannot find anything to do with its money. Chapter 5 asks which.
The capital structure barely moved. Total liabilities fell from 38.1 per cent to 35.6 per cent and equity rose from 61.9 to 64.4. Orontes is financed about two-thirds by its owners, and that did not change much in a year when the balance sheet grew by a fifth.
Goodwill shrank. It was 3.6 per cent of assets and is now 0.9 per cent, because of the impairment you recorded in Part 1. A reader who did not know that would see only the percentage and would have to go to the notes.
WORKED EXAMPLE   Worked example: three lines of the common-size column
Step 1. Cash, 2025. 7,302,100 ÷ 31,672,600 = 23.1 per cent. Round to one decimal, as the whole column does.
Step 2. Cash, 2024. 2,100,000 ÷ 26,445,000 = 7.9 per cent. Note that the base changed: each year is divided by its own total, never by the earlier total.
Step 3. Total current liabilities, both years. 4,280,000 ÷ 31,672,600 = 13.5 per cent, and 3,570,000 ÷ 26,445,000 = 13.5 per cent. The dollar amount rose by $710,000 and the percentage did not move at all.
Check. Step 3 is the point of the technique. In dollars, current liabilities grew. As a share of the company they stood still, because the company grew by the same proportion. The two readings are both true and they answer different questions.
