2.3  The common-size income statement
On the income statement the base is net sales, and the column reads as the fate of each sales dollar. Figure F502-04 is Orontes's.
Figure F502-04. Orontes Foods, Inc. — common-size income statement, year ended December 31, 2025 (percentage of net sales)
@table
Orontes Foods Inc. (whole USD) | 2025 | %
Net sales | 42,000,000 | 100.0
Cost of goods sold | (27,300,000) | (65.0)
Gross profit | 14,700,000 | 35.0
Selling, general and administrative | (7,237,000) | (17.2)
Credit loss expense | (63,000) | (0.1)
Research and development | (500,000) | (1.2)
Impairment losses | (2,724,000) | (6.5)
Total operating expenses | (10,524,000) | (25.1)
Operating income | 4,176,000 | 9.9
Interest expense | (390,000) | (0.9)
Equity-method income | 177,000 | 0.4
Total other income and (expense) | (217,200) | (0.5)
Income before income taxes | 3,958,800 | 9.4
Income tax expense | (989,700) | (2.4)
Net income | 2,969,100 | 7.1
@end
Read it as a sentence. Of every dollar Orontes sold, 65 cents went to the cost of the goods themselves and 35 cents remained as gross profit. Of that 35 cents, 17 went to selling and administration and 6.5 cents went to the impairment. Nine point nine cents survived as operating income, and after interest and tax, seven cents became net income.
The impairment line is why this statement has to be read carefully. A reader comparing Orontes with another food manufacturer would see operating income of 9.9 per cent and might conclude that Orontes is the weaker business. But 6.5 of the 25.1 points of operating expense is a write-down of goodwill and a brand, which will not happen again next year. Without it, operating income would have been 6,900,000 ÷ 42,000,000 = 16.4 per cent. Which of the two figures to use is a real question, not a trick, and Chapter 17 is about how to decide it.
EXAM TRAP   Percentages of a negative number
Costs are shown in parentheses on the statement and their percentages are shown in parentheses too. Do not change the sign halfway down a column.
Gross profit percentage is gross profit over net sales, not cost of goods sold over net sales. Those two add to 100.
If a question gives you a common-size income statement and asks for a dollar amount, you need net sales. Without the base you cannot go back.
WORKED EXAMPLE   Worked example: going back from percentages to dollars
Step 1. A competitor's common-size income statement shows gross profit of 28.0 per cent and net sales of $55,000,000. Gross profit in dollars is 55,000,000 × 0.28 = 15,400,000.
Step 2. Its cost of goods sold is therefore 100.0 − 28.0 = 72.0 per cent of sales, or 55,000,000 × 0.72 = 39,600,000.
Step 3. Compare with Orontes, whose gross profit is 35.0 per cent. On $42,000,000 of sales that is 42,000,000 × 0.35 = 14,700,000.
Check. The competitor sells more and earns less gross profit in dollars: $15,400,000 against $14,700,000 on sales a third larger. The common-size column made the comparison possible; the dollars make it concrete.
