2.4  Reading the two statements together
Each statement answers a different question, and the two together answer a third.
The balance sheet column says what the company is made of. The income statement column says what happens to a sale. Put them side by side and you can see whether the shape of the assets matches the shape of the costs.
Orontes keeps 46.7 per cent of its assets in plant. A company with that much plant has substantial fixed costs, and you would expect the cost of goods sold to contain depreciation and factory overhead that do not fall when sales fall. Part 1 recorded depreciation of $2,100,000 for the year, which is 5.0 per cent of net sales. That is the link: half a dollar of every ten in sales is a charge the company owes to assets it bought years ago, whatever this year's sales turn out to be.
Figure F502-05 states the pairing that an analyst looks for.
Figure F502-05. What a pair of common-size columns suggests
@table
On the balance sheet | On the income statement | What the pair suggests
A large share in plant | A large share in cost of goods sold that does not move with sales | Fixed costs: profit swings hard with volume
A large share in receivables | A credit loss expense that is rising | Selling to customers who pay slowly or not at all
A large share in inventory | A falling gross profit margin | Goods that are not selling, discounted to move
A large share in cash | Interest and dividend income that is small | Cash earning almost nothing
A large share in equity | A small interest expense | Financed by owners, so little fixed charge
@end
The fifth row describes Orontes. Equity is 64.4 per cent of the balance sheet and interest expense is 0.9 per cent of sales: $390,000 against operating income of $4,176,000. The company is carrying very little fixed financial cost. Chapter 10 measures exactly how much room that leaves.
LANGUAGE FOCUS   "Margin" in English
English uses margin for several different things: gross profit margin, contribution margin, margin of safety, and the margin a broker lends against. Arabic هامش and French marge are just as broad. In Section A, margin always means a percentage of sales unless the words say otherwise.
