2.5  Comparing with a company you do not own
The reason to make a common-size statement is to compare. Orontes against itself last year is the easy comparison. Orontes against Barada Wholesale is the instructive one, because Barada is a distributor and Orontes is a manufacturer, and the shapes of the two businesses are different in ways the percentages expose at once.
Figure F502-06 sets selected lines side by side.
Figure F502-06. Orontes and Barada, selected common-size lines, 2025
@table
Line | Orontes (manufacturer) | Barada (distributor)
Inventory, per cent of total assets | 7.7 | 34.0
Property, plant and equipment, per cent of total assets | 46.7 | 9.0
Accounts receivable, per cent of total assets | 13.1 | 28.0
Cost of goods sold, per cent of net sales | 65.0 | 84.0
Gross profit, per cent of net sales | 35.0 | 16.0
@end
Nothing in that table says one company is better run than the other. It says they are different businesses. A distributor buys finished goods and resells them, so its cost of goods sold is most of its sales and its gross margin is thin; it carries little plant and a great deal of stock and receivables. A manufacturer converts raw material, so it earns a wider margin and ties its money up in machinery.
This is the discipline the common-size statement teaches. The percentages are a description, and the judgment comes afterwards, from knowing what the business does. Comparing Barada's 16 per cent gross margin with Orontes's 35 per cent and concluding that Barada is failing would be a serious error, and it is one the exam tests.
EXAM TRAP   Comparable means comparable
Compare a company with companies in the same business, or with itself.
Where the businesses differ, say what the difference is before drawing a conclusion from the percentages.
A difference in accounting policy — LIFO against FIFO, leased plant against owned plant — changes the percentages without changing the business.
