Chapter summary
Figure F502-07 brings the chapter together.
Figure F502-07. Chapter 2 summary map: the key rule for each section.
A common-size statement expresses every line as a percentage of one base: total assets for the balance sheet, net sales for the income statement. The base is fixed by convention and each year is divided by its own total. Orontes's column shows a company whose current assets rose from 31.7 to 44.8 per cent of the balance sheet, almost entirely because cash went from 7.9 to 23.1 per cent, while its capital structure barely moved and remains about two-thirds equity. Its income statement shows 65 cents of every sales dollar going to cost of goods sold and 7.1 cents surviving as net income, with 6.5 points of the operating expense being an impairment that will not recur. Reading the two columns together links the shape of the assets to the shape of the costs. The percentages are a description, not a verdict: a distributor and a manufacturer differ for reasons that have nothing to do with how well either is run.
Practice
Answer the multiple-choice questions first. Then try the case-style set and the written task. The answers explain why each wrong option is wrong, so read them even when you are right.
Multiple-choice questions
P2-01  A common-size balance sheet expresses each line as a percentage of:
A.	net sales.
B.	total assets.
C.	total equity.
D.	the prior-year amount.
P2-02  Use Figure F502-02. Cash as a percentage of total assets at December 31, 2025 is closest to:
A.	7.9 per cent
B.	13.5 per cent
C.	23.1 per cent
D.	44.8 per cent
P2-03  Use Figure F502-04. Orontes's gross profit margin for 2025 is:
A.	65.0 per cent
B.	35.0 per cent
C.	25.1 per cent
D.	9.9 per cent
P2-04  A company's current liabilities rise in dollars but stay at the same percentage of total assets. This means:
A.	total assets rose by about the same proportion.
B.	the company repaid debt.
C.	the percentages are wrong.
D.	current assets fell.
P2-05  A competitor reports net sales of $55,000,000 and a gross profit margin of 28 per cent. Its cost of goods sold is:
A.	15,400,000
B.	39,600,000
C.	27,500,000
D.	cannot be determined
P2-06  Which of the following would NOT change a company's common-size income statement?
A.	A change from FIFO to LIFO
B.	An impairment of goodwill
C.	Selling the same mix of goods at the same margins to twice as many customers
D.	A rise in the selling and administrative expense ratio
P2-07  Orontes's property, plant and equipment fell from 60.5 to 46.7 per cent of total assets while the dollar amount fell by $1,198,000. The main cause of the percentage change is:
A.	the sale of a factory.
B.	growth in total assets, chiefly cash.
C.	an impairment of plant.
D.	a change in depreciation method.
P2-08  On a common-size income statement, an analyst sees cost of goods sold rise from 62 to 65 per cent of sales. Which other movement would be consistent with goods that are not selling?
A.	Inventory falling as a percentage of total assets
B.	Inventory rising as a percentage of total assets
C.	Receivables falling as a percentage of total assets
D.	Cash rising as a percentage of total assets
P2-09  Use Figure F502-06. Which statement about Barada and Orontes is supported by the figures?
A.	Barada is less profitable because its managers are weaker.
B.	Barada ties up more of its assets in inventory and receivables than Orontes does.
C.	Orontes has more customers.
D.	Barada has more plant.
P2-10  Excluding the impairment, Orontes's operating income would be $6,900,000. As a percentage of net sales that is:
A.	9.9 per cent
B.	16.4 per cent
C.	25.1 per cent
D.	35.0 per cent
Case-style practice: a buyer looks at Orontes
A Gulf food group is considering an offer for Orontes. Its analyst has Figure F502-02 and Figure F502-04 and nothing else. She writes three sentences in her file: "Nearly a quarter of the company is cash. The business is two-thirds financed by its owners. Operating margin is under ten per cent, which is low for this industry."
Required. For each of the three sentences, say whether the common-size statements support it as written, and if not, what the analyst would have to add or check. Then name the one line in Figure F502-04 that most affects her third sentence, and say by how much it affects it. Write in continuous prose, under 250 words.
Answers and explanations are at the end of the chapter.
Answers and explanations
Section checks
SC2-1  Answer B. The balance sheet is common-sized on total assets.
A is wrong. Total equity is one of the lines expressed, not the base.
C is wrong. Total current assets is a subtotal, not the base.
D is wrong. Net sales is the base for the income statement.
SC2-2  Answer C. Every line of a common-size income statement, including interest expense, is a percentage of net sales.
A is wrong. Operating income is itself expressed against net sales.
B is wrong. Income before income taxes is also expressed against net sales.
D is wrong. Total assets is the balance sheet base.
SC2-3  Answer B. Assets equal liabilities plus equity, and both sides are divided by the same total assets, so both sides come to 100 per cent.
A is wrong. Rounding affects the last decimal, not the equality.
C is wrong. Equity is included; it is part of the second side.
D is wrong. There is no weighting; each line is divided by one base.
SC2-4  Answer B. Cash moved from 7.9 to 23.1 per cent, a change of 15.2 points, larger than any other line.
A is wrong. Inventory moved from 8.7 to 7.7, one point.
C is wrong. Receivables moved from 14.2 to 13.1, about one point.
D is wrong. Goodwill moved from 3.6 to 0.9, under three points.
SC2-5  Answer B. The percentage holds only when the numerator and the base grow at about the same rate.
A is wrong. The dollar amount rose by $710,000.
C is wrong. The base rose; each year uses its own total.
D is wrong. The percentages are correct to one decimal in both years.
SC2-6  Answer C. On its own a falling percentage shows only that the line is a smaller share of a larger total.
A is wrong. Nothing in the percentage says an asset was sold.
B is wrong. Depreciation exceeding capital spending is one possible cause, but the percentage alone does not show it.
D is wrong. Impairment is one possible cause and the percentage alone does not show it.
SC2-7  Answer B. Gross profit is 35.0 per cent of net sales, so 35 cents of each dollar.
A is wrong. 9.9 cents is operating income.
C is wrong. 65.0 cents is the cost of the goods, not what remains.
D is wrong. 7.1 cents is net income.
SC2-8  Answer B. Gross profit margin is 100 − 72 = 28 per cent.
A is wrong. 72 per cent is the cost, not the margin.
C is wrong. Operating expenses come after gross profit and do not affect it.
D is wrong. 100 per cent is net sales itself.
SC2-9  Answer C. Both figures are defensible; what is not defensible is using one without saying so.
A is wrong. Using the reported figure without noting the impairment misleads the comparison.
B is wrong. Excluding a charge without disclosing the exclusion is the same error in the other direction.
D is wrong. The company is comparable once the charge is identified.
SC2-10  Answer B. Plant generates depreciation and factory overhead, which stay when volume falls.
A is wrong. That describes a business whose costs are variable.
C is wrong. Credit loss expense relates to receivables, not plant.
D is wrong. Interest expense relates to borrowing, not plant.
SC2-11  Answer A. Equity carries no contractual charge, and interest is under one per cent of sales.
B is wrong. Leverage is low, not high, at 35.6 per cent liabilities.
C is wrong. Orontes has $6,000,000 of long-term debt.
D is wrong. Low leverage suggests the opposite.
SC2-12  Answer B. A distributor's margin is thin by the nature of the business.
A is wrong. The margins do not measure management quality across different businesses.
C is wrong. A distributor cannot raise prices above what the market allows for resold goods.
D is wrong. Nothing suggests misstatement.
SC2-13  Answer B. A distributor's assets are mostly the goods it holds for resale and the amounts its customers owe.
A is wrong. Size does not change proportions.
C is wrong. A write-down would be disclosed and is not implied.
D is wrong. Cost flow assumptions change the amount somewhat, not the structure of the business.
SC2-14  Answer A. Leasing keeps plant off the asset base and changes the percentages while the operations are identical.
B is wrong. Selling more units changes the business.
C is wrong. Market size is not a reporting difference.
D is wrong. Headcount is not a reporting difference.
