Multiple-choice questions
P2-01  Answer B. Total assets is the base for the balance sheet.
A is wrong. Net sales is the income statement base.
C is wrong. Total equity is one of the lines expressed.
D is wrong. Comparison with the prior year is horizontal analysis.
P2-02  Answer C. 7,302,100 ÷ 31,672,600 = 23.1 per cent.
A is wrong. 7.9 per cent is the 2024 figure.
B is wrong. 13.5 per cent is total current liabilities.
D is wrong. 44.8 per cent is total current assets.
P2-03  Answer B. Gross profit of 14,700,000 ÷ 42,000,000 = 35.0 per cent.
A is wrong. 65.0 per cent is cost of goods sold.
C is wrong. 25.1 per cent is total operating expenses.
D is wrong. 9.9 per cent is operating income.
P2-04  Answer A. A constant percentage with a rising numerator means the base rose proportionately.
B is wrong. Repayment would reduce the dollar amount.
C is wrong. The arithmetic is consistent.
D is wrong. Current assets are not the base.
P2-05  Answer B. Cost of goods sold is 72 per cent of sales: 55,000,000 × 0.72 = 39,600,000.
A is wrong. This is gross profit, 28 per cent.
C is wrong. This is half of sales, which no figure supports.
D is wrong. Net sales and the margin are enough.
P2-06  Answer C. Doubling volume at the same mix and margins leaves every percentage unchanged.
A is wrong. A change of cost flow assumption changes cost of goods sold and therefore the percentages.
B is wrong. An impairment adds an expense line and changes the percentages.
D is wrong. A change in the expense ratio is a change in the percentages.
P2-07  Answer B. Total assets grew by more than $5,000,000, chiefly cash, so an almost unchanged PP&E is a smaller share.
A is wrong. A factory sale would reduce PP&E by far more than $1,198,000.
C is wrong. The impairments in Part 1 were of goodwill and a brand.
D is wrong. A method change would be disclosed and would not produce a shift of this size in one year.
P2-08  Answer B. Goods that are not selling accumulate, so inventory rises as a share of assets while the margin falls.
A is wrong. Falling inventory suggests goods are moving.
C is wrong. Receivables relate to collection, not to unsold stock.
D is wrong. Rising cash is unrelated to unsold stock.
P2-09  Answer B. Barada holds 34 per cent in inventory and 28 per cent in receivables, against 7.7 and 13.1 for Orontes.
A is wrong. The figures describe different businesses, not different managers.
C is wrong. Nothing in the table concerns customer numbers.
D is wrong. Barada holds 9 per cent in plant against Orontes's 46.7.
P2-10  Answer B. 6,900,000 ÷ 42,000,000 = 16.4 per cent.
A is wrong. 9.9 per cent is the reported figure, after the impairment.
C is wrong. 25.1 per cent is total operating expenses.
D is wrong. 35.0 per cent is gross profit.
Case-style set: a buyer looks at Orontes
A good answer takes the three sentences in turn.
The first sentence is supported exactly. Cash is 23.1 per cent of total assets, which is nearly a quarter, and the figure needs no qualification. What the analyst should add is the question it raises: a manufacturer holding that much cash is either saving for something or failing to invest, and the statement of cash flows and the notes would say which.
The second sentence is supported. Total stockholders' equity is 64.4 per cent of total assets, so the company is financed roughly two-thirds by its owners and one-third by its creditors.
The third sentence is supported as arithmetic and misleading as analysis. Operating income is 9.9 per cent of net sales. But the impairment losses of $2,724,000, or 6.5 points of sales, are a write-down of goodwill and a brand rather than a cost of this year's trading. Excluding them, operating income would be $6,900,000, or 16.4 per cent of sales. The analyst's conclusion about the industry turns almost entirely on that one line, and the difference it makes is 6.5 percentage points.
